Pattern	"lpr-3 was transcribed in external (cuticle secreting) epithelia, including the epidermis, excretory duct and pore cells, and vulva cells, as well as in the gut, but not in the excretory canal cell. Expression began prior to ventral enclosure and persisted throughout larval development, but then disappeared in adults."
Reference	"WBPaper00052963"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00012261"
Reporter_gene
Transgene	"WBTransgene00026164"

Expr_pattern : "Expr14486"
GO_term	"GO:0030112"
Gene	"WBGene00012261"
Reference	"WBPaper00052963"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00012261"
Reporter_gene
Subcellular_localization	"ssSfGFP:: LPR-3 fusion protein accumulated in apical extracellular compartments, including the extraembryonic space and the excretory duct\/pore, rectal, and vulval lumens. ssSfGFP::LPR-3 also associated with the larval cuticle and accumulated between the old and new cuticles before and during molting. However, LPR-3 was not associated with the outer surface of old cuticle or with shed cuticle after molting, and it completely disappeared in adults. We infer that LPR-3 is present within a juvenile glycocalyx layer, and that the connection between this layer and the mature cuticle is severed during molting. In the developing vulva tube, where resolution is highest and the glycocalyx is 2-3 &mu;m thick (Gill et al. 2016), ssSfGFP:: LPR-3 localized specifically to a narrow region along the apical membrane. ssSfGFP:: LPR-3 localized apically to a PH::mcherry membrane marker, confirming that it is extracellular. ssSfGFP::LPR-3 localized slightly more membrane-proximal than the ZP protein LET-653, indicating that it is present in one of the inner layers of the glycocalyx."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00026165"

Expr_pattern : "Expr14487"
GO_term	"GO:0005576"
Gene	"WBGene00002393"
Reference	"WBPaper00052963"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00002393"
Reporter_gene
Subcellular_localization	"LPR-1::SfGFP was only variably visible in the embryonic excretory duct\/pore lumen (Pu et al. 2017), which is open to the extraembryonic environment, but was more robustly observed in the developing vulval lumen, which is a closed compartment. LPR-1::SfGFP filled the entire vulva lumen and did not appear to associate specifically with any glycocalyx layer."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00026166"

