Expr_pattern : "Expr1604"
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0008594" Partial
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0008594" Remark "HAM-1 is restricted to the posterior half of the HSN/PHB neuroblast."
Antibody	"A mouse polyclonal antiserum to a His-tagged HAM-1 fusion protein expressed in E. coli was generated and used to stain whole mounts of wild-type embryos and larvae."
Antibody
Antibody_info	"WBAntibody00000132"
GO_term	"GO:0005737"
GO_term	"GO:0071944"
Gene	"WBGene00001820"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000010"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000013"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000016"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000017"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000018"
Pattern	"HAM-1 is first expressed at the onset of gastrulation, approximately 100 minutes after cleavage of the zygote. At this time, HAM-1 often localizes to the periphery of cells in crescent shaped patterns that are restricted to one side of dividing cells. HAM-1 expression persists until the 1.5 fold stage of embryonic development, approximately 430 minutes after cleavage of the zygote. No HAM-1 expression can be detected later during embryonic or larval hermaphrodite development."
Pattern	"HAM-1 protein is expressed in many mitotic and postmitotic cells. To determine whether HAM-1 is expressed in the lineage that produces the HSN and PHB neurons, embryos were stained at 260-300 minutes after cleavage of the zygote. HAM-1 is restricted to the posterior half of the HSN\/PHB neuroblast. After division of the HSN\/PHB neuroblast, HAM-1 ring staining can occasionally be seen in cells that, by position, could be the HSN\/PHB precursor. This staining, however, is usually not detectable suggesting that HAM-1 is degraded rapidly after division of the HSN\/PHB neuroblast."
Reference	"WBPaper00002607"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00001820"
Subcellular_localization	"HAM-1 expressed in cells at metaphase. Chromosomes are condensed and HAM-1 is distributed to one side of the cell. HAM-1 expressed in cells at telophase. Two nuclear envelopes have reformed and HAM-1 is distributed to the side of the cell that will be inherited with the posterior nucleus. HAM-1 staining also appears as rings that surround cells."
Subcellular_localization	"HAM-1 often localizes to the periphery of cells in crescent shaped patterns that are restricted to one side of dividing cells."

