Expr_pattern : "Expr2384"
Antibody	"Polyclonal rabbit antibody against SAS-4. Amino acids 350-517 of SAS-4 were expressed with pGEX-6P-3 (Pharmacia) and injected into two rabbits according to standard procedures (Eurogentec). Antibodies were affinity purified with a Hitrap NHS column (Pharmacia) coupled to the antigen or a strip of denatured fusion protein blotted onto nitrocellulose. Anti-SAS-4 antibodies were stored at ~0.13 ug\/ml. Antibodies from both rabbits and purification methods showed similar staining patterns."
Antibody
Antibody_info	"WBAntibody00000608"
GO_term	"GO:0005737"
GO_term	"GO:0005813"
Gene	"WBGene00004726"
Reference	"WBPaper00005765"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00004726"
Remark	"Cold-induced depolymerization of the microtubule network does not alter SAS-4 distribution at the centrosome."
Remark	"Transgenic animals expressing GFP-SAS-4 were generated. Staining of transgenic embryos with anti-GFP antibodies confirmed the findings made with anti-SAS-4 antibodies."
Subcellular_localization	"Anti-SAS-4 antibodies label a tiny dot in the center of centrosomes, as well as in the cytoplasm, to a lesser extent. Both centrosomal and cytoplasmic staining are reduced by ~60% in sas-4(RNAi) embryos, indicating that RNAi only partially depletes SAS-4 but that both localizations reflect bona fide SAS-4 distribution."
Subcellular_localization	"SAS-4 is present at centrosomes throughout the cell cycle in early embryos. While a single dot can be detected per MTOC during most of the cell cycle, two dots become visible per MTOC toward the end of mitosis. As this coincides with the onset of centrosome duplication, the appearance of two dots likely reflects splitting of the mother\/daughter centrioles. Furthermore, a tiny dot was observed next to the male pronucleus immediately after fertilization, suggesting that SAS-4 is present with the paternally contributed centrioles. Staining of sperm confirmed that SAS-4 is present as a tiny dot in the perinuclear region, where centrioles are known to reside. In fact, a tiny dot of SAS-4 is present in male germ cells throughout spermatogenesis."

