Pattern	"The body-wall muscle staining became apparent at the 3.5-fold stage of embryogenesis, was most apparent in late embryos and L1 stage larvae, and persisted to adulthood. The vulval muscles, predominantly the four Vm1 cells, and the intestinal muscles also displayed GFP fluorescence. Weaker fluorescence was observed in the anal depressor and anal sphincter muscles. GFP expression was also observed in 1215 head neurons in each animal, including in the SIADL, SIADR, SIAVL, and SIAVR neurons."
Pattern	"The body-wall muscle staining became apparent at the 3.5-fold stage of embryogenesis, was most apparent in late embryos and L1 stage larvae, and persisted to adulthood. The vulval muscles, predominantly the four Vm1 cells, and the intestinal muscles also displayed GFP fluorescence. Weaker fluorescence was observed in the anal depressor and anal sphincter muscles. GFP expression was also observed in head neurons in each animal, including in the SIADL, SIADR, SIAVL, and SIAVR neurons."
Reference	"WBPaper00006135"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00006318"
Remark	"When expressed in a sup-9(lf);unc-93(gf) mutant, the sup-9::gfp reporter could restore the rubberband Unc phenotype caused by an unc-93(gf) allele, indicating that this reporter fusion protein is functional."
Reporter_gene
Subcellular_localization	"SUP-9::GFP expression was observed along the surface of body-wall muscle cells, with a punctate stripe pattern characteristic of dense bodies, structures functionally analogous to vertebrate Z-lines, which connect the myofibril lattice to the cell membrane. Additional staining was observed in muscle arms, which project from the muscle body toward neurons to form synapses."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00000319"

