In_Situ
Life_stage	"WBls:0000005"
Life_stage	"WBls:0000010"
Pattern	"A hybridization signal was first detected in the nucleus of the E cell at the 8-cell stage, indicating the onset of zygotic expression of tbx-9 in this cell. Expression in the E cell was retained in its daughter cells Ea and Ep until the 24-cell stage. It then became undetectable. At this stage, signals were detected in five more cells."
Pattern	"After this stage, signals were observed in a subset of cells at each stage up to the approximately 400-cell stage and became undetectable just before the onset of morphogenesis. The most intensive hybridization signals were found in four cells at the approximately 200-cell stage. These cells were arranged as two pairs of two cells near the center of embryos, a location usually occupied by MS descendant cells."
Pattern	"For exact orientation, embryos were subjected to double staining by fluorescence in situ hybridization for the tbx-9 mRNA along with marker mRNAs. Two of the tbx-9 expressing cells were identified as the Ca and Cp cells. Double staining by fluorescence in situ hybridization for tbx-9 and elt-1 revealed the remaining three tbx-9 expressing cells were AB descendant cells destined for hypodermal cells."
Reference	"WBPaper00006477"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00006546"

Expr_pattern : "Expr2975"
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0005733"
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0005792"
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0005813" Uncertain
GO_term	"GO:0005634"
Gene	"WBGene00006546"
Pattern	"When observed by GFP fluorescence, TBX-9 was detected in a subset of cells,which were considered to be not only the cells containing the tbx-9 mRNA, but also their descendant cells. During morphogenesis,when the tbx-9 mRNA was no longer detected, the TBX-9 protein was observed in dorsal hypodermal cells, lateral hypodermal cells, ventral hypodermal cells, and intestinal cells. GFP fluorescence in the hypodermis and intestine lasted until the early part of the L1 larval stage, then it disappeared. Body-wall muscle cells derived from the MS cell are located in anterior and mid-ventral regions of the body. GFP signals observed near hypodermal cells in the head may be MS-derived body-wall muscle cells."
Reference	"WBPaper00006477"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00006546"
Reporter_gene
Subcellular_localization	"Notably,TBX-9::GFP fluorescence was exclusively observed in the nucleus, indicating strict nuclear localization of the protein."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00001016"

