Pattern	"CEP-1 is abundant in mitotically dividing distal germ cells. Upon the entry into meiotic prophase in the transition zone, CEP-1 protein is completely absent. CEP-1 reappears in late meiotic pachytene cells and remains up to the diplotene\/early diakinesis stage."
Reference	"WBPaper00024954"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00000467"
Remark	"Gene_regulation: There is a reciprocal relationship between CEP-1 and GLD-1 protein levels, as CEP-1 is only high where GLD-1 levels are low, consistent with the hypothesis that GLD-1 might repress the translation of cep-1 mRNA."
Subcellular_localization	"Subcellularly, CEP-1 is localized to the nucleoplasm and the concentration and\/or localization of CEP-1 is apparently unaffected by ionizing radiation."

