Subcellular_localization	"The subcellular localization of CHAT-1 in the intestine was determined by expressing CHAT-1::CFP under control of the intestine-specific promoter vha-6 (Pvha-6chat-1::cfp). CHAT-1 was localized to both plasma membranes and intracellular tubular and vesicular structures and colocalized with TAT-1. The intestinal tubular and vesicular localization pattern was also observed when the expression of CHAT-1 was controlled by the endogenous promoter."
Subcellular_localization	"To determine the identities of the cytosolic compartments labeled by CHAT-1, mCHERRY fusions of different endocytic markers were coexpressed together with CHAT-1::GFP and TAT-1 (Pvha-6chat-1::gfp +Pvha-6tat-1). TAT-1 was included to ensure efficient ER export of CHAT-1::GFP; this combination is subsequently referred to as CHAT-1::GFP for simplicity. CHAT- 1::GFP displayed a tubular and vesicular staining pattern, which did not overlap with either the Golgi marker MANS or the lysosomal marker Lysotracker Red, indicating that CHAT-1 is not on the Golgi or mature lysosomes. No CHAT-1::GFP was found on the RAB-7-positive ring-like structures, suggesting that it is not enriched on late endosomes or early lysosomes. CHAT-1 partially overlapped with RAB-5 on punctate structures, but not on tubule-like structures that were negative for mCHERRY::RAB-5. Thus, a proportion of CHAT-1\/ TAT-1 may localize to RAB-5-positive early endosomes. CHAT-1\/TAT-1 associates with the tubular membrane of early endosomes and ERCs. CHAT-1::GFP overlapped with mRFP::RME-1 on basolateral tubulo-vesicular structures, indicating that CHAT-1 also localizes to RME-1- positive recycling endosomes."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00031023"

