Pattern	"rgef-1 promoter activity is evident in a high proportion of neurons in four independently isolated strains. GFP is not detected in nonneuronal cells. Terminal divisions and differentiation of neurons are completed before rgef-1 promoter activity is switched on during late embryonic development. Panneuronal GFP fluorescence is sustained from the end of embryogenesis (hatching) through adulthood. The rgef-1 promoter is active throughout the nervous system. GFP is evident in head neurons, nerve ring, tail neurons, and nerve cord."
