Pattern	"RAB-6.2::GFP is highly expressed in body wall muscles, pharyngeal and vulval muscles, hypodermis, intestine, the gonad, coelomocytes, seam cells, vulval epithelia, distal tip cells, ventral cord dendrites, and neurons. We also introduced this transgene into nematodes that also express a Pglr-1::monomeric RFP (mRFP) transgene (Shim et al., 2004), and we found that RAB-6.2::GFP is expressed in the GLR-1-expressing command interneurons."
Reference	"WBPaper00040563"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00004270"
Reporter_gene
Subcellular_localization	"RAB-6.2 is localized to punctate structures in the neuron cell body and along the ventral cord dendrites. We introduced the Pglr-1::cerulean:: rab-6.2 transgene into nematodes that express the Golgi resident protein mannosidase (MANS)::YFP (Rolls et al., 2002; Glodowski et al., 2005) and observed nearly complete colocalization between Cerulean::RAB-6.2 and MANS::YFP, indicating that RAB-6.2 is localized on or near Golgi structures."
Transgene	"WBTransgene00031307"

Expr_pattern : "Expr9917"
Anatomy_term	"WBbt:0005175" Certain
Antibody	"The HIM-5 antibody was made by Strategic Diagnostics Inc. (Newark, DE) as part of the modENCODE project using the ~100 C- terminal amino acids of the protein."
Antibody
GO_term	"GO:0000793"
Gene	"WBGene00001864"
Reference	"WBPaper00040572"
Reflects_endogenous_expression_of	"WBGene00001864"
Subcellular_localization	"HIM-5 protein is found associated with chromosomes from the mitotic region of the germline until late pachytene. HIM-5 can be seen at low levels in the mitotic zone, accumulates in the transition zone and early pachytene, and decreases mildly upon entry into pachytene and more dramatically in late pachytene. No staining is observed outside of the gonad. In the germlines of wild type hermaphrodites, HIM-5 preferentially localizes to most meiotic chromosomes but is clearly excluded from one pair. To determine if HIM-5 is also under-represented on the X, hermaphrodite germlines were co-stained with anti- XND-1 and anti-HIM-5 antibodies. XND-1 and HIM-5 co-localize to the same subset of chromosomes, indicating that HIM-5 is also enriched on autosomes."

